sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The very possibility of detailed mathematical analysis of a system tends to imply that its output has at least some deviations from perfect randomness, whereas simple rules not constrained by the need for such analysis can generate highly random behavior.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Amenability to mathematical analysis implies deviations from randomness";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Intrinsic Generation of Randomness” (from p. 315), p. 321. Scope: deterministic systems used to generate randomness, including pseudorandom generators"
}
